#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:2
msgid "Development Mode (a.k.a. \"Editable Installs\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:4
msgid ""
"When creating a Python project, developers usually want to implement and "
"test changes iteratively, before cutting a release and preparing a "
"distribution archive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:7
msgid ""
"In normal circumstances this can be quite cumbersome and require the "
"developers to manipulate the ``PYTHONPATH`` environment variable or to "
"continuously re-build and re-install the project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:11
msgid ""
"To facilitate iterative exploration and experimentation, setuptools allows "
"users to instruct the Python interpreter and its import machinery to load "
"the code under development directly from the project folder without having "
"to copy the files to a different location in the disk. This means that "
"changes in the Python source code can immediately take place without "
"requiring a new installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:18
msgid ""
"You can enter this \"development mode\" by performing an :doc:`editable "
"installation <pip:topics/local-project-installs>` inside of a :term:`virtual "
"environment`, using :doc:`pip's <pip:cli/pip_install>` ``-e/--editable`` "
"flag, as shown below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:22
msgid ""
"$ cd your-python-project\n"
"$ python -m venv .venv\n"
"# Activate your environment with:\n"
"#      `source .venv/bin/activate` on Unix/macOS\n"
"# or   `.venv\\Scripts\\activate` on Windows\n"
"\n"
"$ pip install --editable .\n"
"\n"
"# Now you have access to your package\n"
"# as if it was installed in .venv\n"
"$ python -c \"import your_python_project\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:37
msgid ""
"An \"editable installation\" works very similarly to a regular install with "
"``pip install .``, except that it only installs your package dependencies, "
"metadata and wrappers for :ref:`console and GUI scripts <console-scripts>`. "
"Under the hood, setuptools will try to create a special :mod:`.pth file "
"<site>` in the target directory (usually ``site-packages``) that extends the "
"``PYTHONPATH`` or install a custom :doc:`import hook <python:reference/"
"import>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:44
msgid ""
"When you're done with a given development task, you can simply uninstall "
"your package (as you would normally do with ``pip uninstall <package "
"name>``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:47
msgid ""
"Please note that, by default an editable install will expose at least all "
"the files that would be available in a regular installation. However, "
"depending on the file and directory organization in your project, it might "
"also expose as a side effect files that would not be normally available. "
"This is allowed so you can iteratively create new Python modules. Please "
"have a look on the following section if you are looking for a different "
"behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:54
msgid "Virtual Environments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:56
msgid ""
"You can think about virtual environments as \"isolated Python runtime "
"deployments\" that allow users to install different sets of libraries and "
"tools without messing with the global behaviour of the system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:60
msgid ""
"They are a safe way of testing new projects and can be created easily with "
"the :mod:`venv` module from the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:63
msgid ""
"Please note however that depending on your operating system or distribution, "
"``venv`` might not come installed by default with Python. For those cases, "
"you might need to use the OS package manager to install it. For example, in "
"Debian/Ubuntu-based systems you can obtain it via:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:68
msgid "sudo apt install python3-venv"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:72
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can also try installing :pypi:`virtualenv`. More "
"information is available on the Python Packaging User Guide on :doc:`PyPUG:"
"guides/installing-using-pip-and-virtual-environments`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:77
msgid ""
"Editable installation hooks implemented according to :pep:`660`. Support "
"for :pep:`namespace packages <420>` is still **EXPERIMENTAL**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:83
msgid "\"Strict\" editable installs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:85
msgid ""
"When thinking about editable installations, users might have the following "
"expectations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:88
msgid ""
"It should allow developers to add new files (or split/rename existing ones) "
"and have them automatically exposed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:90
msgid ""
"It should behave as close as possible to a regular installation and help "
"users to detect problems (e.g. new files not being included in the "
"distribution)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:93
msgid ""
"Unfortunately these expectations are in conflict with each other. To solve "
"this problem ``setuptools`` allows developers to choose a more *\"strict\"* "
"mode for the editable installation. This can be done by passing a special "
"*configuration setting* via :pypi:`pip`, as indicated below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:98
msgid "pip install -e . --config-settings editable_mode=strict"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:102
msgid ""
"In this mode, new files **won't** be exposed and the editable installs will "
"try to mimic as much as possible the behavior of a regular install. Under "
"the hood, ``setuptools`` will create a tree of file links in an auxiliary "
"directory (``$your_project_dir/build``) and add it to ``PYTHONPATH`` via a :"
"mod:`.pth file <site>`. (Please be careful to not delete this repository by "
"mistake otherwise your files may stop being accessible)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:110
msgid ""
"Strict editable installs require auxiliary files to be placed in a ``build/"
"__editable__.*`` directory (relative to your project root)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:113
msgid ""
"Please be careful to not remove this directory while testing your project, "
"otherwise your editable installation may be compromised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:116
msgid ""
"You can remove the ``build/__editable__.*`` directory after uninstalling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:120
msgid ""
"Added new *strict* mode for editable installations. The exact details of how "
"this mode is implemented may vary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:126
msgid "Limitations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:128
msgid ""
"The *editable* term is used to refer only to Python modules inside the "
"package directories. Non-Python files, external (data) files, executable "
"script files, binary extensions, headers and metadata may be exposed as a "
"*snapshot* of the version they were at the moment of the installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:133
msgid ""
"Adding new dependencies, entry-points or changing your project's metadata "
"require a fresh \"editable\" re-installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:135
msgid ""
"Console scripts and GUI scripts **MUST** be specified via :doc:`entry-points "
"</userguide/entry_point>` to work properly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:137
msgid ""
"*Strict* editable installs require the file system to support either :wiki:"
"`symbolic <symbolic link>` or :wiki:`hard links <hard link>`. This "
"installation mode might also generate auxiliary files under the project "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:140
msgid ""
"There is *no guarantee* that the editable installation will be performed "
"using a specific technique. Depending on each project, ``setuptools`` may "
"select a different approach to ensure the package is importable at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:143
msgid ""
"There is *no guarantee* that files outside the top-level package directory "
"will be accessible after an editable install."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:145
msgid ""
"There is *no guarantee* that attributes like ``__path__`` or ``__file__`` "
"will correspond to the exact location of the original files (e.g., "
"``setuptools`` might employ file links to perform the editable "
"installation). Users are encouraged to use tools like :mod:`importlib."
"resources` or :mod:`importlib.metadata` when trying to access package files "
"directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:150
msgid ""
"Editable installations may not work with :doc:`namespaces created with "
"pkgutil or pkg_resources <PyPUG:guides/packaging-namespace-packages>`. "
"Please use :pep:`420`-style implicit namespaces [#namespaces]_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:154
msgid ""
"Support for :pep:`420`-style implicit namespace packages for projects "
"structured using :ref:`flat-layout` is still **experimental**. If you "
"experience problems, you can try converting your package structure to the :"
"ref:`src-layout`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:158
msgid ""
"File system entries in the current working directory whose names "
"coincidentally match installed packages may take precedence in :doc:"
"`Python's import system <python:reference/import>`. Users are encouraged to "
"avoid such scenarios [#cwd]_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:162
msgid ""
"Setuptools will try to give the right precedence to modules in an editable "
"install. However this is not always an easy task. If you have a particular "
"order in ``sys.path`` or some specific import precedence that needs to be "
"respected, the editable installation as supported by Setuptools might not be "
"able to fulfil this requirement, and therefore it might not be the right "
"tool for your use case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:169
msgid ""
"Editable installs are **not a perfect replacement for regular installs** in "
"a test environment. When in doubt, please test your projects as installed "
"via a regular wheel. There are tools in the Python ecosystem, like :pypi:"
"`tox` or :pypi:`nox`, that can help you with that (when used with "
"appropriate configuration)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:177
msgid "Legacy Behavior"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:179
msgid ""
"If your project is not compatible with the new \"editable installs\" or you "
"wish to replicate the legacy behavior, for the time being you can also "
"perform the installation in the ``compat`` mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:183
msgid "pip install -e . --config-settings editable_mode=compat"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:187
msgid ""
"This installation mode will try to emulate how ``python setup.py develop`` "
"works (still within the context of :pep:`660`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:191
msgid ""
"The ``compat`` mode is *transitional* and will be removed in future versions "
"of ``setuptools``, it exists only to help during the migration period. Also "
"note that support for this mode is limited: it is safe to assume that the "
"``compat`` mode is offered \"as is\", and improvements are unlikely to be "
"implemented. Users are encouraged to try out the new editable installation "
"techniques and make the necessary adaptations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:202
msgid "How editable installations work"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:204
msgid "*Advanced topic*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:206
msgid ""
"There are many techniques that can be used to expose packages under "
"development in such a way that they are available as if they were installed. "
"Depending on the project file structure and the selected mode, "
"``setuptools`` will choose one of these approaches for the editable "
"installation [#criteria]_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:211
msgid ""
"A non-exhaustive list of implementation mechanisms is presented below. More "
"information is available on the text of :pep:`PEP 660 <660#what-to-put-in-"
"the-wheel>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:214
msgid ""
"A static ``.pth`` file [#static_pth]_ can be added to one of the directories "
"listed in :func:`site.getsitepackages` or :func:`site.getusersitepackages` "
"to extend :obj:`sys.path`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:217
msgid ""
"A directory containing a *farm of file links* that mimic the project "
"structure and point to the original files can be employed. This directory "
"can then be added to :obj:`sys.path` using a static ``.pth`` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:220
msgid ""
"A dynamic ``.pth`` file [#dynamic_pth]_ can also be used to install an "
"\"import :term:`finder`\" (:obj:`~importlib.abc.MetaPathFinder` or :obj:"
"`~importlib.abc.PathEntryFinder`) that will hook into Python's :doc:`import "
"system <python:reference/import>` machinery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:226
msgid ""
"``Setuptools`` offers **no guarantee** of which technique will be used to "
"perform an editable installation. This will vary from project to project and "
"may change depending on the specific version of ``setuptools`` being used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:233
msgid "Debugging Tips"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:235
msgid ""
"If encountering problems installing a project in editable mode, follow these "
"recommended steps to help debug:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:238
msgid ""
"Try to install the project normally, without using the editable mode. Does "
"the error still persist? (If it does, try fixing the problem before "
"attempting the editable mode)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:241
msgid ""
"When using binary extensions, make sure all OS-level dependencies are "
"installed (e.g. compilers, toolchains, binary libraries, ...)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:243
msgid ""
"Try the latest version of setuptools (maybe the error was already fixed)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:244
msgid ""
"When the project or its dependencies are using any setuptools extension or "
"customization, make sure they support the editable mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:247
msgid ""
"After following the steps above, if the problem still persists and you think "
"this is related to how setuptools handles editable installations, please "
"submit a `reproducible example <https://stackoverflow.com/help/minimal-"
"reproducible-example>`_ at `the bug tracker <https://github.com/pypa/"
"setuptools/issues>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:254
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:256
msgid ""
"You *may* be able to use *strict* editable installations with namespace "
"packages created with ``pkgutil`` or ``pkg_namespaces``, however this is not "
"officially supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:261
msgid ""
"Techniques like the :ref:`src-layout` or tooling-specific options like "
"`tox's changedir <https://tox.wiki/en/stable/config.html#conf-changedir>`_ "
"can be used to prevent such kinds of situations (checkout `this blog post "
"<https://blog.ganssle.io/articles/2019/08/test-as-installed.html>`_ for more "
"insights)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:268
msgid ""
"``setuptools`` strives to find a balance between allowing the user to see "
"the effects of project files being edited while still trying to keep the "
"editable installation as similar as possible to a regular installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:273
msgid ""
"i.e., a ``.pth`` file where each line correspond to a path that should be "
"added to :obj:`sys.path`. See :mod:`Site-specific configuration hook <site>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/development_mode.rst:277
msgid ""
"i.e., a ``.pth`` file that starts where each line starts with an ``import`` "
"statement and executes arbitrary Python code. See :mod:`Site-specific "
"configuration hook <site>`."
msgstr ""
